From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: the pointer's place to the guest while another app is in front

What: with the VM in full screen on two or more displays, a move over one
of its windows while another app is in front gives the guest the pointer's
place, and nothing else (ui/omacvm-pointer-hover.h says when). The app in
front keeps the pointer and the keyboard: no grab, no corner guard (it
detaches and moves the Mac's cursor), the Mac's cursor stays as it is (shown,
over the guest's pointer, as at boot), no window becomes key and nothing is
activated. A click still takes the pointer and brings OmacVM.app to the
front, as before. The windows of the other displays already see moves while
another app is in front (their tracking area is active always): their early
return for such a move now gives the place first. The main window's
tracking area is only active in the key window, so its view gets a second
one, active always, with its own owner (omacvmHoverMain): it goes through
the same rule, which needs OmacVM not in front, so no move comes twice.
That area sees every move over the main view, also with the pointer
grabbed: a grabbed pointer, OmacVM in front and one display are dropped
first, before the rule's input (which asks the WindowServer).
OMACVM_POINTER_HOVER=0: as before. qemu.log at start: "cocoa: pointer: a
move over the VM on two or more displays while another app is in front:
its place to the guest (...)" or "dropped (OMACVM_POINTER_HOVER=0)"; with
OMACVM_POINTER_DEBUG=1 "omacvm-pointer: hover (output N): ..." at most every
10 s.

Why: the user had the VM in full screen on the MacBook's display and an LG
monitor and switched the MacBook's display to macOS. A Mac app came to the
front there and QEMU let go of the pointer (as it should). From then on the
LG's window dropped every move (837b4a88: a move over another display's
window takes the pointer only while OmacVM is in front, so a pointer that
only passes over the VM does not take the keyboard from the Mac app): no
hover and the guest's pointer stood still on the LG, scrolls went to the
old place, only a click brought it back (2026-10-28, with the pointer
layer on; it is not about the layer).

Pause drawing while hidden: each move now reaches the guest while the
main window's display is paused; the VM's display agent holds Hyprland's
wake on moves off meanwhile (same release), else every move would wake the
paused display.

Upstream: not applicable (OmacVM's display windows).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-pointer-hover.sh (the rule; the wiring
on the patched ui/cocoa.m; --self-test: every wiring check fails on the code
without it).
